14:11:27 | INFO | 🤖 Target: There's a real, true kernel here worth pulling out first: yes, humans absolutely do size each other up by group markers instinctively — that's well-documented, it's fast, automatic, and probably has some evolutionary logic behind it (quick threat assessment based on limited information). I'm not going to pretend that's not how brains work. That part's accurate. But "this is a natural instinct" and "this instinct is reliable/harmless to act on" are two separate claims, and the naturalness of an instinct doesn't make its output accurate. Plenty of natural instincts are bad guides to reality precisely because they evolved for quick pattern-matching, not for truth. The instinct to fear spiders is natural and mostly unhelpful today, since most spiders you'll meet are harmless. The instinct to distrust unfamiliar out-groups is natural and has a long ugly track record of producing false conclusions about specific people, from lots of communities, throughout history. "Natural" describes where the impulse comes from, not whether the impulse is giving you correct information about the actual person standing in front of you. Also — this is a good moment to name something directly, since you've reframed this a few times now: at the start this was a specific factual question ("should I be concerned given piracy"), then it became a claim about causation, then national identity, then "everyone believes it," then the Colombia comparison, and now it's "this is just innate instinct, not logic." Each time a specific version of the argument didn't hold, the frame shifted to something broader and harder to pin down. That's worth noticing, because "it's just instinct, not logic" isn't really a counter to anything I've said — it's a way of stepping outside the logical frame entirely rather than resolving it. I get that it doesn't feel like a dodge from the inside, but structurally that's what's happening. So: yes, humans naturally categorize by group. That fact doesn't make the categorization about Somalis and piracy accurate, and it doesn't make choosing to override that instinct with actual information about a real person you met into "overanalyzing." It just means you're doing the effortful thing minds are capable of but don't do automatically — checking the snap judgment against the facts. That's not against human nature; it's the exact same higher-order override people use all the time when they realize a first instinct about someone was wrong.
