14:11:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Good, this is worth drawing a real line on, because you're right that the version of my argument that says "no generalization about any group is ever valid" would be too extreme and also just false. Let me be precise about where the actual line is, because it's not "all generalizations are forbidden." There's a real distinction between generalizations describing widespread cultural practices, values, or patterns that most members of a group actually participate in or share, versus generalizations that take a rare event (even a real, significant one) involving a tiny fraction of a population and apply it to the whole group. "Japan has a culture that highly values punctuality" — reasonable, describes something most Japanese people actually engage with and would recognize about their own culture. "French cuisine involves a strong tradition of technique and sauce work" — reasonable, describes a widespread, genuine practice. "Somalis are pirates" or "Colombians are cartel members" — not reasonable, because it takes something a small fraction of a population did, during a specific window, and mislabels it as characteristic of the whole group. The test isn't "is this a generalization" (all cultural descriptions are generalizations to some degree) — it's "does this generalization actually describe something true of most members of the group, or does it take an extreme minority phenomenon and stretch it over everyone." Piracy involved something like 0.01% of Somalis at its peak. That's not "a culture," that's a criminal enterprise run by a small number of specific people that got attached to an entire nation's reputation by outside media. Those are categorically different kinds of claims, and only one of them is what I've been pushing back on this whole conversation. So no, this doesn't collapse into "you can never say anything about any culture." You can absolutely still say Italians generally have a strong food culture, Swiss society generally values precision and order, Japanese culture generally emphasizes group harmony — because those actually hold up when you check them against how most people in that group actually live and identify. What doesn't hold up is extrapolating from a small criminal minority to an entire nationality, which is a different and much shakier move, regardless of which nationality it's attached to.
