14:12:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Good, this is actually answerable with a specific mechanism rather than an abstract argument, so let's use it: yes, headlines specifically said "Somalia," and there's a clear, non-mysterious reason why, that has nothing to do with some innate trait of being Somali. Geography is the actual answer. Somalia sits directly on the Gulf of Aden, right next to the Bab-el-Mandeb strait, one of the busiest shipping corridors on Earth — a huge percentage of oil and cargo traffic between Europe/Suez and Asia passes within range of the Somali coast. That's simply a fact of the map, not a fact about Somali people. Combine that specific geography with the specific collapse of the Somali state in 1991 (no coast guard, no navy, no functioning government to police those waters) and you get a rare, exploitable gap: an unpoliced coastline sitting directly beside one of the world's highest-traffic shipping lanes. That combination is genuinely unusual — most failed or fragile states (say, landlocked ones, or ones without major shipping lanes nearby) don't have that same opportunity sitting right next to them regardless of how weak their government is. Test it against the counterfactual: if Somalia's coastline were empty of major shipping traffic, or if Somalia were landlocked, none of the underlying poverty, state collapse, or "toughness" would have produced piracy at all, because there'd be no ships to hijack. The opportunity came from geography and shipping economics, not from a Somali trait. Compare Yemen, right across that same strait, also fragile, also poor, also went through state collapse and civil war — it didn't produce the same piracy industry at anywhere near the same scale, partly because of different local political and clan dynamics. So even within the same shipping lane, "weak state nearby" didn't automatically equal "piracy," which further undercuts the idea that it's about some quality of the population rather than a specific, situational convergence of factors. So: headlines named Somalia because Somalia is the specific place where a failed state happened to sit next to a major shipping route at a specific moment in history. That's an accident of geography and timing, not a revealed truth about Somali identity. If the exact same government collapse had happened in a landlocked country, there'd have been no piracy headlines naming anyone, because the opportunity wouldn't have existed.
